 "overview": {
  "summary": "The precision analog half of the high-voltage pair, and the board a 1100 V or 2.2 A fault usually ends at. A15 carries two heated-substrate hybrids and the two resistor networks that set the accuracy of three ranges: HR7 with its 70 kohm / 7 Mohm network, which is both the 100:1 dc amplifier for the 1100 V dc range and the 100:1 ac attenuator for the 1100 V ac sense path, and H4 with the 57 ohm / 0.57 ohm network Z5, which gives the 2.2 A range its gain of 100 over A7's 22 mA range. Between them sit the series-pass and current-limit circuit that tells A14's magnitude loop how hard to drive the high-voltage supplies, the 35 mA current limit, the HV OUT / HV SENSE protection clamp, and the AC CAL path through the 399.6 kohm Z6. What A15 does not have is any digital control of its own: all thirteen of its relays -- K1 to K12 and K14 -- and every FET control line are driven from A14, and its four diagnostic points are multiplexed on A14. It cannot be made to do anything without A14 working.",
  "connectors": "P601 and P602, DIN 41612 type C, 64-pin right angle, into J601 and J602 on the Analog Motherboard.",
  "sections": "§2-155 … §2-166",
  "note": "The board carries the 1100 V output node. HV OUT and HV SENSE both come onto it, three high-voltage cable assemblies (W1, 8.125 in; W2 and W3, 7.375 in) run across it, and TP2 is a dedicated high-voltage socket. §5-21 has you remove the rear shield MP3 and run the board on an extender card, so all of that is exposed and live."
 },
